<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taipei</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Taihoku</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taipei is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, in the Taipei Basin in the north of the island. It grew in the 18th and 19th centuries from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> settlements and was walled by the Qing in the 1880s. Under Japanese rule from 1895 to 1945 it was the colonial capital, Taihoku, and since 1949 it has been the seat of the government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taipei.jpg" alt="Taipei" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 毛貓大少爺 · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Taipei_Skyline_2022.06.29.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Taipei lies in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, mostly on the Taipei Basin, an ancient lakebed. The basin is bounded by the narrow valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a> and Xindian rivers, which join to form the Tamsui River along the city's western border. The city is an enclave of the municipality of New Taipei City and sits about 25 kilometres southwest of the port city of Keelung. In the north the terrain rises to Qixing Mountain, 1,120 metres high, the highest dormant volcano in Taiwan, in Yangmingshan National Park.</p><p>The spellings Taipei and Tʻai-pei derive from the Wade–Giles romanisation, and the name means the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in Chinese. During Japanese rule from 1895 to 1945 the city was known as Taihoku, the Japanese reading of the same two characters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the influx of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> colonists, the Taipei Basin was inhabited mainly by the Ketagalan, a Plains Indigenous people. The number of Han colonists grew gradually from the early 18th century under Qing rule, after the government began permitting development in the area. In 1875 the northern part of the island was incorporated into the new Taipeh Prefecture. Taipeh-fu was the temporary capital of the island in 1887, when it was declared a province, Fukien-Taiwan Province, and it was formally made the provincial capital in 1894.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in 1895, the name was changed to Taihoku and the city was administered under Taihoku Prefecture. The Japanese rulers embarked on an extensive programme of urban planning that featured extensive railway links, and a number of landmarks and cultural institutions date from this period. The National Taiwan Museum, the island's oldest, was founded by the colonial government on 24 October 1908. Taipei has been the national seat of the central government of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> since 1949, and in 1967 it became a special municipality under the Executive Yuan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Taipei is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, and the municipality had an estimated population of 2,494,813 in March 2023. It forms the core of the Taipei–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keelung">Keelung</a> metropolitan area, with New Taipei and Keelung, which has 7,047,559 inhabitants. The city is the political, economic, educational and cultural centre of Taiwan.</p><p>Its landmarks include the Chiang Kai-shek Memorial Hall, the Lungshan Temple of Manka, the Zhinan Temple, the National Palace Museum and the Presidential Office Building, around which stands Ketagalan Boulevard, a site of parades and public gatherings. Natural features include Maokong, Yangmingshan and hot springs. Because of the island's ambiguous political status, the name Chinese Taipei is used for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> in international organisations and sporting events such as the Olympics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Taipei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As Communist armies advanced, the government moved from Nanjing to Guangzhou and Chongqing and, in December 1949, to <b>Taipei</b>, while between one and two million soldiers and civilians followed it to Taiwan, which Japan had ruled from 1895 to 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-china">Republic of China · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1933 the threat of Japanese invasion forced the evacuation of its treasures; after the war part returned, but in 1948 the Nationalist government shipped a selection to Taiwan, where it became the core of the National Palace Museum in <b>Taipei</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/forbidden-city">Forbidden City · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>A magnitude 7.0 earthquake off northern Taiwan seriously damaged Keelung and <b>Taipei</b>, and its tsunami drowned hundreds; the death toll was estimated at 580.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Japanese troops landed at the end of May; Tang Jingsong fled to the mainland and <b>Taipei</b> fell in early June.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-formosa">Republic of Formosa</a></li><li><blockquote>A governor-general with wide powers ruled from <b>Taihoku</b> (Taipei).</blockquote><a href="/state/taiwan-under-japanese-rule">Taiwan under Japanese rule</a></li></ul></section>
